// game.ron — tuning: Vector Gear, camera, physics.
//
// All lengths in METERS (1 Bevy unit = 1 meter, docs/conventions.md).
// The backlog counts in studs; the conversion happens HERE, once, at 0.28 m/stud
// (docs/QUESTIONS.md Q-002) — there are no studs in the code.
//
// ⚠️ UNTUNED. These numbers are starting values, not balancing. That is exactly what this
// file is for: changing them costs no rebuild. The P1 gate is a blind test against the
// reference work, and until that is passed nothing here is finished (bible 6.2).
//
// ⚠️ EXCEPTION: whatever comes from assets/data/scale.ron is NOT untuned — it is given by
// the user (2026-08-09) and only mirrored here so the game systems have a number.
// Affected are player.height_m, player.eye_height_m, camera.fov_deg and
// vector.hook_range_m. tests/data.rs falls over the moment one of them drifts away from
// scale.ron. **A direct figure in meters from the user beats any derivation** — including
// the conversion out of the backlog (Q-002).
(
    // 60 Hz fixed simulation. The image interpolates in between; over the network a
    // frame-dependent result is not a comfort problem, it is desync (init.md §6 rule 4).
    simulation_hz: 60.0,
    // Solver substeps per simulation step. avian's own default is 6
    // (avian3d-0.7.0/src/dynamics/solver/schedule.rs:185-191) — and that is measurably too
    // few for this game. 24 is the ONLY measured value that holds both ends at once: the
    // swing loses 4.26 %/s instead of 8.97 %/s at 6, and the worst wall penetration over 18
    // measured cases stays at -0.0043 m of the -0.01 m allowed. It costs 0.72 ms/tick with
    // 20 players in a 401-body city against a 4 ms budget.
    // NOT untuned — this number was measured. Whoever lowers it measures again.
    substeps: 24,
    gravity_m_s2: -20.0,   // harder than 9.81: momentum play, not simulation

    player: (
        // = scale.ron:reference.human_height_m. The user writes "check the capsule exactly!"
        // — so EXACTLY, not roughly: every size comparison in the image hangs on this number.
        height_m: 1.8,
        radius_m: 0.35,

        // -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
        // F-010 — the ground slide: „Gleit-Ausweichmanoever am Boden mit I-Frames und
        // Momentum-Erhalt. Slide vermeidet Stomp-Angriff; geht fliessend in Sprint ueber."
        // -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
        // ⚠️ UNTUNED, and the one verb in this round that costs **no gas at all**. That is a
        // decision and not an omission: gas is what the GEAR burns (gear.ron, `vector.gas_*`),
        // and a slide is legs. Billing it would make the one move a player has left on an empty
        // tank cost the thing he has run out of, which is the shape of a death spiral. What
        // bounds it instead is `slide_cooldown_s` and the fact that you must already be moving.
        //
        // 12 m/s against run_speed_m_s: a slide is faster than a run and slower than a swing
        // (17-21 m/s, Q-018), so it reads as a burst and never as traversal. And it is a FLOOR,
        // not a speed: `player::locomotion::slide` keeps the larger of this and what you had,
        // which is the *„Momentum-Erhalt"* half of the row — landing at 30 m/s and sliding
        // keeps 30.
        slide_speed_m_s: 12.0,
        // 0.55 s = 33 ticks of slide. Long enough to cross the 7 m street the size table gives
        // (12 m/s x 0.55 = 6.6 m), short enough that it is a move and not a stance.
        slide_duration_s: 0.55,
        // 0.30 s = 18 ticks, and deliberately **shorter than the slide itself**: the tail of
        // the slide is the part that carries you out from under the foot, and i-frames that
        // last the whole movement are a dodge nobody has to time. 55 % of the duration.
        slide_iframes_s: 0.30,
        // Measured from the tick the slide STARTS, so it includes the slide: 0.9 - 0.55 = 0.35 s
        // of standing between two. A held C is then one slide per 54 ticks, never a stance.
        slide_cooldown_s: 0.9,
        // You cannot slide from standing. 3 m/s is half a walk — enough that a player who is
        // actually moving always gets it, enough that a stationary tap does nothing.
        slide_min_speed_m_s: 3.0,
        // P5. ⚠️ UNTUNED. Two ways to lose is the bar (docs/PLAN-GAME.md §1), and one of
        // them is this number reaching zero. 100 against a husk's damage of 34 means
        // **three strikes and you are down** — which is what makes a telegraphed wind-up
        // worth reading instead of tanking. A downed player is a STATE with a timer, not a
        // removed entity (shared::MovementState::Downed).
        health: 100.0,
        run_speed_m_s: 6.0,
        // 6.5 m/s at g = −20 give 6.5²/(2·20) = **1.056 m of apex**. That is enough for
        // neither a door (2.10 m; that would take 9.17 m/s) nor the eaves of the small
        // house (3.00 m). **Without gas you get on top of nothing in this city** — for a
        // Vector Gear game that is right, but it is a decision and not a side effect, and
        // that is why it stands here.
        jump_speed_m_s: 6.5,
        // F-006 air control. ⚠️ UNTUNED. Stood in Rust as `-gravity_m_s2 / 2` until
        // 2026-08-12 (docs/FINDINGS.md FIND-051) — a derivation is still a game value, and §4
        // says a game value lives here. **10.0 is half of -gravity_m_s2, deliberately: WASD
        // alone can never hold you up**, so what keeps a player airborne stays the rope and the
        // gas (docs/NEXT.md §1f, „bis das gas ausgeht"). Two bounds come out of that and both
        // survive the move: strictly **below 20** (= -gravity_m_s2), or gasless hovering is
        // free — tests/data.rs falls over on that one — and well below vector.boost_m_s2: 34,
        // or Shift stops being the strong option (10 is 29 % of it today).
        // ⚠️ Coupled: tests/player.rs::f014_the_input_still_steers_the_carried_momentum asserts
        // > 10° of turn and measures 11.22° — the margin is 1.22°, so anything below ≈10.6
        // takes that test red. That is the guard working, not flake.
        air_accel_m_s2: 10.0,
        air_accel_empty_fraction: 0.5,
        // What is left of it with an empty tank. ⚠️ UNTUNED, and **not a derivation — the
        // user's spec**: „ohne gas kann man immernoch w a d nutzen um etwas movement aufzubauen
        // (aber hälfte ca)" (docs/NEXT.md §1e). So the air control is not gated on gas, it only
        // gets weaker, and an empty tank stops being the dead end behind „seile ohne boost
        // bringen gar nichts". Dimensionless — no unit suffix.

        // ⚠️ THE NEXT THREE ARE NEW ON 2026-08-13 AND NOTHING READS THEM YET. They are the
        // numbers of the mixing rule (docs/NEXT.md §1B), which `W4` builds in
        // src/player/locomotion.rs. They stand here first, alone, so that the keys are frozen
        // before four agents start consuming them — and so that a missing one crashes on load
        // instead of being defaulted (§4: no serde(default) for a game value).
        //
        // The user, 2026-08-12: „wenn ich mit seilen festhake ... und w in die richtung drücke
        // will ich dass man deutlich mehr geboosted wird. also dass man dort richtig
        // hingezogen wird. wenn man aber a oder d drückt wird nach links/rechts geboostet!"
        //
        // ⚠️ UNTUNED. What `W` adds ON TOP of air_accel_m_s2 when a rope is anchored, pulling
        // along the rope direction. **3x the free-air control**, and both bounds are real:
        //   - strictly ABOVE air_accel_m_s2 (10), or „deutlich mehr geboosted" is a lie and
        //     the key buys nothing a rope-less player does not already have;
        //   - at most boost_m_s2 (34), or the free `W` outruns the boost you pay gas for and
        //     Shift stops being the strong option — the same bound air_accel_m_s2 carries,
        //     one rung higher. 30 is 88 % of the boost: unmistakably strong, still second.
        // tests/data.rs holds both ends.
        air_pull_m_s2: 30.0,
        // ⚠️ UNTUNED. What `A`/`D` add on top when a rope is anchored — on the horizontal
        // right vector, NOT the rope tangent (a tangent flips sign when the anchor passes
        // beside you and would invert the strafe mid-swing, docs/NEXT.md §1B).
        //
        // The bound is a SUM and that is the point: air_accel_m_s2 + air_lateral_m_s2 <= 34,
        // i.e. holding `D` alone may never beat one boost. 10 + 18 = 28 leaves 6 m/s² of head
        // room. Below air_accel_m_s2 it would not read as a „boost" at all; the floor is
        // therefore the same 10.
        air_lateral_m_s2: 18.0,
        // ⚠️ UNTUNED. How far above min_rope_m the rope pull has already faded to zero, in
        // metres of rope length. **This key exists because of a measured cliff, not a feeling:**
        // FIND-035 — at min_rope_m the constraint takes 17 m/s out of the player in ONE tick.
        // Thrusting straight at an anchor you are already 3 m from feeds exactly that cliff, so
        // the pull lets go before it: full strength at 15 m of rope, zero at 3 m.
        //
        // Two bounds, both derived and both in tests/data.rs:
        //   - >= 2 * min_rope_m (6 m), or the fade is shorter than the cliff it exists to avoid;
        //   - <= 0.1 * hook_range_m (50 m), or a "near the anchor" special case is running over
        //     a tenth of every rope in the game and the pull is no longer what steers a swing.
        air_pull_fade_m: 12.0,
        // ── THE LOOK-PULL, 2026-08-20 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        // The user, after playing: „man muss wenn man sich hookt und in die richtung gehen
        // stärker in die richtung gehen! also wenn man da hin schaut dass nicht alle physics
        // also gravitiy so stark sind. dass man gerader hingezogen wird. damit es sich gut
        // anfühlt. damit man gut steuern kann damit. aber wenn man nicht hinschaut man auch
        // gut kreise schwingen kann"
        //
        // **How much of `gravity_m_s2` the rope pull cancels — at PERFECT ALIGNMENT ONLY.**
        // It rides the very same cosine `cᵢ = max(0, l̂ · r̂ᵢ)` the pull itself rides, and the
        // same near-anchor fade, so it is a FUNCTION OF THE ANGLE and not a second constant:
        // look at your anchor and gravity nearly lets go, look off the rope and it is back at
        // the full −20 the arc is built on. That is his two clauses, one term.
        //
        // ⚠️ It could not be done by moving `boost_rope_fraction`. That key blends a
        // DIRECTION, and at perfect alignment look and rope are the same direction — the blend
        // is a no-op exactly where he wants the change. What was measured instead
        // (docs/FINDINGS.md FIND-131, `scripts/f005-feel.txt` ACT 3): hanging under the church
        // nave, looking straight at an anchor 9.5 m above him, `W` held for four seconds,
        // he climbed **1.996 m**. The arithmetic says why and it is not close: the rope term
        // gives 30·0.983 = 29.5 m/s² along a rope that is 0.552 vertical and the look term
        // 10 m/s² along a look that is 0.559 vertical, so **21.9 m/s² of lift against 20.0 of
        // gravity** — 1.9 m/s² net. The pull was never weak; gravity was eating 91 % of it.
        //
        // ⚠️ UNTUNED. Two bounds, and
        // tests/player.rs::f005_the_gravity_relief_is_a_fraction_between_the_droop_and_weightlessness
        // holds both:
        //   - **> 0.5**, or the aligned haul still loses more than half of the pull and the
        //     approach still droops — 0.0 is the 1.996 m measured above;
        //   - **< 1.0**, or a player looking at his anchor with `W` down is WEIGHTLESS, and
        //     there is then no arc to fall back into when he looks away — which is the half of
        //     his sentence that asks for the swing to survive.
        // At 0.7 the net climb of that same measurement becomes 15.7 m/s² and 6.0 m/s² of
        // weight is left over, so he still settles into a swing the moment the look leaves the
        // rope. **Costs no new gas key**: it is part of the rope term, and the rope term is
        // already `GasConsumer::Steer` — on an empty tank it is zero with the rest of it.
        air_pull_lift_fraction: 0.7,
        // 1.65 -> 1.60: the user gives the camera height directly
        // (scale.ron:camera.height_m). The old 1.65 was estimated from the body height;
        // a stated figure beats an estimate.
        eye_height_m: 1.6,
        // Largest distance per substep of the integrator. At max_speed_m_s = 75 a 60 Hz
        // tick covers 1.25 m — without substeps the player drives through every wall, and
        // only sometimes. Has to be strictly smaller than world.min_wall_m, or
        // tests/data.rs falls over. 0.25 m give 5 substeps at top speed.
        max_substep_m: 0.25,
        // ⚠️ UNTUNED, and INVENTED — no source, no measurement of its own. The reasoning:
        // on the ground the horizontal velocity is written directly (src/player/locomotion.rs),
        // so friction brakes nothing you want braked; what it WOULD brake is a swinging
        // player who grazes a wall, and a measurement puts that at 75 % of the speed per
        // second at a combined 0.65. The player therefore carries 0.0 and the combine rule
        // `Min`, so that no wall can put its own friction on him.
        // The consequence of `Min`: at exactly 0.0 this knob pins the combined value to 0,
        // whatever the wall carries. Above 0.0 it is a real knob again.
        friction: 0.0,
        // ⚠️ UNTUNED, but not invented: 0.0 is avian's own default and means "does not
        // bounce". A player who rebounds off a wall is a bug, not a feature.
        restitution: 0.0,
        // ⚠️ UNTUNED and INVENTED. How steep a surface may be and still count as ground.
        // **Measures nothing today:** every body in this world is an axis-aligned cuboid, so
        // every contact normal is exactly ±X, ±Y or ±Z, and every value strictly between 0
        // and 90 gives the identical result. The number becomes real the day a ramp does.
        max_ground_slope_deg: 50.0,
    ),

    vector: (
        // 112.0 -> 90.0. The 112 came out of 400 studs x 0.28 (Q-002). The user gives the
        // anchor range directly as **90 m** (scale.ron:vector.anchor_range_m), and a direct
        // figure in meters beats any derivation. Consequence for Q-002: 90/400 would be
        // 0.225 m/stud — the factor 0.28 still holds for everything the user has said
        // nothing about, but it has become questionable.
        // 90 -> 200 (scale.ron:vector.anchor_range_m, and tests/data.rs falls over if the two
        // ever drift apart). The user, 2026-08-10, after playing it: *"zudem muss die hook
        // range sehr viel laenger sein!"* — see docs/QUESTIONS.md Q-035 for why 200 and not
        // 400.
        // **200 -> 500 on 2026-08-12, and the number is his, verbatim**: „und das seil muss
        // deutlich deutlich schneller gespannt werden. nicht frame perfekt aber mit ca 500m pro
        // sekunde. mit der range 500 meter!" (docs/NEXT.md §1A). Same precedence rule as the
        // last two times — a direct figure in metres beats every derivation, including his own
        // earlier 90 and 200. It costs world.half_extent_m 600 -> 900, and that price is
        // written out down there rather than re-derived here.
        hook_range_m: 500.0,
        // 90 -> 160. NOT a taste change: it follows from the range. A hook is a projectile,
        // so at the old 90 m/s a shot to the new maximum range would take **2.22 s** to
        // arrive — longer than most of a swing, and long enough that the anchor you aimed at
        // is not where you are any more. 160 m/s holds the worst-case flight at 1.25 s, which
        // is what a 90 m shot cost before (1.0 s) plus a little. ⚠️ UNTUNED.
        //
        // **160 -> 500 on 2026-08-12, and this one is a stated speed, not a consequence**:
        // „mit ca 500m pro sekunde". It happens to be exactly what the range needs — 500/500 =
        // **1.0 s** of worst-case flight, back to what a 90 m shot cost in the very first
        // version — but that is a coincidence worth naming, not the reason. The reason is that
        // he asked for the two halves of one feeling at once: „(was instant sein soll)" and
        // „aber man soll sehen wie es aufspannt". 1 s at full range is fast enough to read as
        // instant at the 20-60 m most shots actually are (0.04-0.12 s) and still slow enough
        // that the line is drawn, not teleported.
        hook_speed_m_s: 500.0,
        // F-001 names „einziehend" as the fourth hook state; game.ron knew only the outward
        // flight so far. Faster than the outward flight, so a missed shot is not punished.
        // ⚠️ Pure starting value, no source (docs/QUESTIONS.md Q-016).
        //
        // **120 -> 500 on 2026-08-12.** The old rule „faster than the outward flight" is dead —
        // the outward flight is 500 now and „faster than instant" is not a number. What
        // replaces it is a bound with a player-visible meaning, and tests/data.rs holds it:
        // **hook_range_m / this <= 1.0 s**, i.e. a miss at maximum range is cleared up inside
        // one second. Above that the punishment for a bad shot is not the miss, it is the wait
        // — and §1A's whole first requirement is that firing again is never blocked.
        // At 500 the retract equals the shot: out and back is 2.0 s worst case, 0.24 s at the
        // 60 m a real miss happens at. ⚠️ UNTUNED.
        hook_retract_speed_m_s: 500.0,
        // ── TIME TO HOOK, 2026-08-20 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        // The user, after playing: „und time to hook also e drücken zum connecten geht zu
        // lang! das muss schneller gehen."
        //
        // **A CEILING ON THE WHOLE FLIGHT, IN SECONDS** — the tip flies at
        // `max(hook_speed_m_s, distance / this)`, so `hook_speed_m_s` still decides every shot
        // that is short enough and this key decides the rest. Measured before it existed
        // (docs/FINDINGS.md FIND-130): press -> `Anchored` is `1 + ceil(d / (500/60))` ticks,
        // i.e. **3 ticks / 50 ms at the 18 m of a roof-to-roof hook** and **61 ticks / 1.02 s
        // at the 500 m the range allows**. The near field was never the complaint; the far
        // field is a second of a game that does nothing you can act on.
        //
        // 0.10 s is not chosen, it is this file's own „reads as instant" band — the hook
        // comment above says a 20-60 m shot at 0.04-0.12 s „reads as instant" in so many
        // words. Two consequences, and both are the point:
        //   * **below 50 m nothing moves at all** (50 / 500 = 0.10 s), so every shot the game
        //     is actually played at keeps the 500 m/s the user named on 2026-08-12;
        //   * at 60 Hz it is **6 frames**, which is the other half of his sentence from that
        //     day — „aber man soll sehen wie es aufspannt". A cap in TIME keeps the line
        //     visible at every range; a higher `hook_speed_m_s` would have made a near shot
        //     a teleport to buy the same second back.
        // Worst case speed at 500 m is 5000 m/s, and that is what a time ceiling means.
        // ⚠️ UNTUNED. Its own bounds are held by
        // tests/vector_hooks.rs::f005_the_flight_ceiling_is_a_number_that_binds_and_a_number_a_player_would_wait
        // — `hook_range_m / hook_speed_m_s > this` (a ceiling that never binds is a key nobody
        // can tell is broken; the control run set it to 10.0 and the LEDGER test stayed green,
        // which is why that second test exists) and `<= 0.15 s`, so the file cannot answer
        // „das muss schneller gehen" by raising its own ceiling.
        hook_flight_max_s: 0.10,
        // ⚠️ THE NEXT FOUR ARE NEW ON 2026-08-13 AND NOTHING READS THEM YET — `W3`
        // ── F-023 IS RETIRED, 2026-08-23 ──────────────────────────────────────────────
        // The user, after playing the reference beside this game: „dann das auseinander mit
        // q und e kann weg. einfach da wo ich hinschau (also fadenkreuz) geht das seil hin."
        //
        // Sixteen keys went with that sentence — aim_spread_deg/_min/_max/_step/_floor/
        // _slew/_settle, the seven aim_sep_* of the metre model, and aim_side_coherence_k —
        // together with the mouse wheel that set the first of them and the settings row that
        // showed it. It overrides his own 2026-08-12 „es muss mehr rechts und links
        // spreaden!!", and the standing rule says it may (CLAUDE.md: his instruction beats
        // his own earlier number). docs/QUESTIONS.md Q-048 · git show 83f09da:assets/data/
        // game.ron for the model that stood here.
        //
        // ⚠️ The ASSIST below is a different feature and it STAYS: it searches sideways from
        // the crosshair along one screen row (FIND-133) and publishes ONE point that both
        // arms fly to. „Sideways from the crosshair" is not „the two arms apart".

        // -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
        // F-024 / F-025 — the anchor candidate system. „es sollte best match sein" (the
        // user, 2026-08-18). Every weight here is the backlog's own number
        // (docs/backlog/gameplay.ron, F-025) and not a taste of the implementation:
        // 45 + 25 + 15 + 10 = 100 % of reward, and a 5 % penalty on top.
        // -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
        // Resolution of the candidate sweep, PER SIDE of the crosshair.
        //
        // The user, 2026-08-19: „die seile sollen immer auf der horzontalen fest sein. also
        // wenn das fadenkreuz 0, 0 ist sollen die seile nur auf der x achse snappen (objekte
        // finden) also seitlich! dann ist es auch besser einzuschaetzen."
        //
        // So the sweep is a LINE on the screen-horizontal through the crosshair, not a cone:
        // 8 rays a side, 16 extra rays for a player at 0.21 us each = 3.4 us a tick against a
        // 16 666 us budget — and only while the assist is switched on at all. That is exactly
        // the budget the 2 rings x 4 probes cone cost before it collapsed; what changed is
        // where the rays go.
        //
        // ⚠️ `PlayerSettings::assist_catch_pct` (0..100 % -> 0..20 deg, src/shared/settings.rs)
        // keeps its meaning and changes its SHAPE: it is now the HALF-WIDTH of a line, the
        // furthest a probe may sit left or right of the crosshair, and no longer the radius of
        // a disc. 100 % is 20 deg to the left and 20 deg to the right, and 0 deg up or down.
        //
        // 8 steps sample the widest catch half-width (20 deg) at 2.5 deg,
        // which is finer than the widest half-fan the aim wheel can open (22 deg), so no
        // reachable anchor sits between two probes on the axis that is left.
        assist_probe_steps: 8,
        // F-025 verbatim: „Winkelabweichung zum Fadenkreuz (Hauptgewicht 45 Prozent)".
        assist_score_angle_w: 0.45,
        // „Momentum-Erhalt (25 Prozent, bevorzugt Punkte, die die aktuelle Flugbahn
        // fortsetzen statt sie zu bremsen)".
        assist_score_momentum_w: 0.25,
        // „Hoehenvorteil relativ zur Bewegungsrichtung (15 Prozent)".
        assist_score_height_w: 0.15,
        // „Distanz im nutzbaren Mittelbereich (10 Prozent)".
        assist_score_distance_w: 0.10,
        // „Abwertung des zuletzt genutzten Punktes (5 Prozent, verhindert Pendeln zwischen
        // zwei Punkten)" — SUBTRACTED from the sum above.
        assist_score_recent_w: 0.05,
        // Below this the momentum term has nothing to say and scores its own midpoint.
        // 4 m/s is under a walk (player.walk_speed_m_s) — a player who is moving at all has
        // a trajectory worth preserving, a player standing on a roof does not.
        // ⚠️ UNTUNED.
        assist_momentum_min_speed_m_s: 4.0,
        // Where the height term saturates. 20 m is roughly two Ashgate houses
        // (house_large 9.9 m) — past that "higher" stops being more useful, it is just far.
        // ⚠️ UNTUNED.
        assist_height_full_m: 20.0,
        // The usable middle of the range. The first hook of a flight is fired at 6-20 m and
        // a chained swing at 30-60 m (FIND-096); 30 m sits in the middle of what is actually
        // flown, far from the 500 m the ray can technically reach. ⚠️ UNTUNED.
        assist_dist_ideal_m: 30.0,
        // Half the band. At 30 +/- 60 m the term is 0, so everything from the hand out to
        // 90 m still scores something and only the far quarter of hook_range_m is written
        // off. ⚠️ UNTUNED.
        assist_dist_span_m: 60.0,
        // How much better a candidate must be than the point the player is really aiming at,
        // at the lowest non-zero strength. Required margin = this * (1 - strength/100), so
        // 100 % is F-024's SNAP (best candidate always wins) and 5 % barely ever fires.
        // 0.25 is a quarter of the whole score range, i.e. "clearly better, not marginally".
        // ⚠️ UNTUNED — this is the knob to move if he says the assist feels too eager.
        assist_margin_full: 0.25,
        // ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        // F-005 Reel-In. **Two mechanisms read this one number, and they are not the same
        // mechanism** (`docs/QUESTIONS.md` Q-050, closed 2026-08-25):
        //
        //   Pendulum  metres per second taken off the rope's LENGTH
        //             (`player::rope::shorten_ropes`, on the joint's `limits.max`). The
        //             player's speed is then whatever the constraint makes of it — a reel
        //             multiplies the tangential velocity by `L_prev/L_new`, which is why ACT 1
        //             of `scripts/game-full.txt` arrived at 28.741 m/s and not at 28.
        //   Drive     metres per second of CLOSING SPEED along the rope
        //             (`player::locomotion::rope_winch`). `Drive` builds no joint at all
        //             (FIND-152), so there is no length to shorten: the winch commands the
        //             rope axis directly and leaves every other axis of the flight alone.
        //             Same script, same act, measured 26.695 m/s and the same 35.000 m roof.
        //
        // ⚠️ The number is NOT re-tuned for the drive, on purpose: the two readings land within
        // 8 % of each other on the one act that has ever been photographed, and a second key
        // would be a second thing to get wrong. If he says the winch is too slow, THIS is the
        // line — it is bounded above only by `max_speed_m_s` and below by `run_speed_m_s`
        // (a winch slower than walking is not a verb).
        reel_speed_m_s: 28.0,
        // ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        // B-003. How much LONGER than it is a `warp` may leave a rope and still keep it.
        //
        // Since 2026-08-10 a warp released EVERY rope, whatever the distance. That is right
        // for the case it was written for (55 m off a 9 m rope = 47.93 m of drag in one tick)
        // and wrong for the 35 scripts that use `warp` to put a player somewhere — it cost the
        // F-029 round two runs and it is documented nowhere a script author looks (FIND-116).
        //
        // The rule is not a taste, it is what the joint does: `limits = (0, L)` corrects only
        // when the distance EXCEEDS L, so a teleport that leaves the rope no longer than it
        // already is has nothing to correct. A warp TOWARD the anchor, along it, or onto the
        // same spot therefore keeps the rope — which is exactly the case that cost F-029 its
        // two runs, a warp that SHORTENED the rope from 62 m to 23 m and let go anyway.
        //
        // ⚠️ **And the other direction has no usable budget at all — measured, 2026-08-19,
        // `tests/vector_rope.rs::probe`, a 9.00 m rope with gravity off:**
        //
        //   excess   drag in one tick   speed the player leaves at
        //   0.0000            0.0000 m                    0.000 m/s
        //   0.0001            0.0024 m                    0.143 m/s
        //   0.0100            0.2401 m                   14.403 m/s
        //   0.0500            1.2001 m                   72.004 m/s
        //   0.2500            1.4479 m                   75.000 m/s   (= vector.max_speed_m_s)
        //
        // The solver corrects the whole excess inside ONE substep, so it comes out as a
        // velocity of `excess * simulation_hz * substeps` = excess * 1440 /s. There is no
        // "small enough to be harmless" metre budget: one centimetre is already 2.4x running
        // speed. So this key is a FLOAT TOLERANCE and not a distance the player may be moved.
        //
        // The bound, held by tests/vector_rope.rs: the kick a kept warp may deliver stays
        // below player.run_speed_m_s (6.0) — slower than the player moves himself —
        // i.e. `<= 6.0 / (60 * 24)` = 0.00417 m. 0.004 is 5.8 m/s of worst case.
        // The first version of this key said 0.25 (derived from player.max_substep_m, which
        // bounds a POSITION step and says nothing about a solver impulse). The table above is
        // what that guess was worth, and it is why the table is in the file.
        warp_rope_slack_m: 0.004,
        // Any closer would drag the camera into the wall — and since 2026-08-25 it is also
        // **where the reel ends under BOTH models**: `shorten_ropes` clamps `limits.max` here,
        // `rope_winch` drops an arm out of its blend here, and `vector::gas` stops billing
        // `gas_reel_per_s` here. One number, one place to end, one place to stop paying.
        min_rope_m: 3.0,
        // ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        // FIND-149 — WHICH FORCE A HOOKED ROPE APPLIES. Pendulum or Drive. **One line.**
        //
        // The user played the reference (Attack on Titan Revolution) on Windows, beside this
        // game, on 2026-08-23:
        //
        //   „wenn ich mich hooke: dann werde ich direkt rangezogen wenn ich ran gehe. mit a und
        //    d kann man zur seite gehen. aber sonst wird man direkt hingezogen! WENN ICH NICHTS
        //    DRUCKE DANN WIRD AUCH NICHT RANGEZOGEN!"   … „aber es ist ein etwas smoother
        //    uebergang! aber recht schnell!"
        //
        // THE REFERENCE DRIVES, IT DOES NOT SWING. The rope is a DIRECTION; the key is the
        // FORCE. Ours is the opposite by construction — an avian DistanceJoint plus gravity IS
        // a pendulum, and that is what every number below `Pendulum` was measured against.
        //
        //   Pendulum  the game as it stood on 2026-08-22, bit for bit. The joint exists, the
        //             B-005 ratchet shortens it, `air_pull_m_s2` steers it, and a player who
        //             lets go of every key still swings. All of tests/vector_rope.rs is a
        //             statement about THIS line.
        //   Drive     no joint at all. `player::locomotion::rope_drive` chases a target
        //             velocity along the rope while a movement key is held. NO KEY, NO PULL —
        //             a hooked player who presses nothing falls exactly as if he had no hook.
        //
        // ⚠️ THE DEFAULT WAS `Pendulum` UNTIL 2026-08-23 AND IT IS `Drive` NOW, ON HIS WORD.
        // He played the drive and asked for MORE of it, not less — „wenn ich mich hooke und w
        // druecke oder generell booste dann soll ich erstmal ziemlich direkt daran gezogen
        // werden. also ziemlich gerade" (FIND-153) — which is the second time he has described
        // the drive's model as the one he wants. The switch stays, so a session can still A/B:
        //
        //     sed -i 's/rope_force_model: Drive/rope_force_model: Pendulum/' assets/data/game.ron
        //
        // ⚠️ Under `Drive` the reel-in (F-005, Ctrl) has nothing to shorten — there is no
        // constraint — so it currently does nothing while still being billed by vector::gas.
        // Q-049 and docs/FINDINGS.md FIND-151 carry that; it is deliberate scope, not an
        // oversight.
        rope_force_model: Drive,
        // The three numbers the drive is made of. **All three are the user's to judge** — they
        // are derived below, not measured, and no test in this repository can tell a good one
        // from a bad one.
        //
        // ⚠️ UNTUNED. The speed W chases along the rope, in m/s. **50.0 until 2026-08-23.**
        //   - a pure swing on the pendulum runs 17-21 m/s (Q-018), so anything at or under that
        //     would make the drive a downgrade and he would feel it instantly;
        //   - at 50 the drive topped out SLOWER than the pendulum it replaces: 52.5 m/s against
        //     75.0 over the same 100 m (FIND-152). „es darf strenger sein" does not survive
        //     being the slower of the two options;
        //   - 🔴 THE CEILING IS NOT THIS KEY. vector.max_speed_m_s (75) is an avian
        //     `MaxLinearSpeed` on the body itself (src/player/mod.rs), so ANY number above it is
        //     a lie the solver silently clips. 70 leaves the ~1.6 m/s of gravity sag and the
        //     free look thrust room UNDER that clamp, so what the player feels is the drive and
        //     not the clip. **Raising the drive past 70 means raising max_speed_m_s first.**
        //   - the reference's own „Gear Shift" setting reads 600 in his menu. Roblox counts
        //     STUDS (1 stud ~ 0.28 m), so that is ~168 m/s if it is a speed at all — more than
        //     twice our own hard clamp, and FIND-149 records it as UNRESOLVED.
        drive_speed_m_s: 52.0,
        // ⚠️ UNTUNED. The time constant of the ramp, in seconds — „ein etwas smoother uebergang!
        // aber recht schnell!" is the whole specification. **0.25 until 2026-08-23, and that
        // quarter second WAS the complaint:** „man macht was und man merkt es auch direkt!"
        //   - THE NUMBER HE FEELS IS TIME TO 90 % OF THE TARGET, integrated once per tick the
        //     way air_control integrates it: 0.25 s cost **567 ms**, 0.08 s costs **167 ms**;
        //   - it is also the whole of „ziemlich gerade". The steady-state sag under gravity is
        //     atan(ramp * g / speed) — 5.71 deg at (0.25, 50), **1.31 deg at (0.08, 70)** — and
        //     the carried momentum that bends a flight into a curve decays with this same
        //     constant. A shorter ramp is a straighter line, exactly;
        //   - LOWER bound 2/simulation_hz = 0.033 s. The drive is integrated explicitly, so a
        //     constant under one tick overshoots instead of ramping. 0.08 s is 4.8 ticks, i.e.
        //     dt/ramp = 0.21 — stable, and still an exponential and not a step;
        //   - UPPER bound: much past 0.5 s and „recht schnell" is gone.
        // Smaller = snappier AND straighter. This is the key for „fuehlt sich traege an".
        drive_ramp_s: 0.08,
        // ⚠️ UNTUNED. What A/D add ACROSS the rope, in m/s — „das a d sorgt dafuer dass man
        // nicht immer direkt zum seil gezogen wird", which is the whole reason the key exists.
        // **18.0 until 2026-08-23**, borrowed from player.air_lateral_m_s2 so that the sideways
        // authority started at the same number in both models — but the UNIT is different (m/s
        // here, m/s^2 there) and 18 against the new 70 is only atan(18/70) = 14.4 deg of
        // deflection, which is not an „ausser ich move nach links".
        //   - 30 m/s buys **23.2 deg** off the straight line at full W+D, and the shared cap
        //     means that is a DIRECTION change and not extra speed;
        //   - A/D alone no longer brakes the flight (they chase their own axis only,
        //     locomotion::rope_drive point 6), so this number is now a steering authority and
        //     nothing else — it used to double as the speed he got dragged back down to;
        //   - bound: <= drive_speed_m_s, or A/D alone would hit the drive's own cap.
        drive_lateral_m_s: 36.0,
        // FIND-172. The acceleration ceiling of the drive, in m/s^2 — and it is the one number
        // in this block that is about WEIGHT rather than about speed.
        //
        // The user, 2026-08-26, in the same breath as „es ist zu aggressiv. man wird zu sehr
        // rangezogen": „zudem fuehlt sich die gravitation nicht richtig an. oder die masse von
        // dem character. es fuehlt sich zu leicht an."
        //   - a velocity drive has NO inertia by construction: `a = (v* - v)/ramp` replaces the
        //     whole velocity in ~3*ramp NO MATTER HOW FAST the player was going. Mass cancels
        //     out (`Forces::apply_linear_acceleration` ignores it on purpose), so nothing in
        //     this game resists a direction change. That is what „zu leicht" is;
        //   - and it is the same thing as „zu aggressiv" read from the other side: unbounded,
        //     the term is speed/ramp from rest = **875 m/s^2 at (70, 0.08)**, i.e. 44 g;
        //   - a ceiling gives it back without touching gravity_m_s2 or the body's mass: a SMALL
        //     correction is under the cap and the ramp alone governs it (so „man merkt es
        //     direkt" survives, FIND-153), while a LARGE change of velocity now takes time in
        //     proportion to how much of it there is. Reversing a 52 m/s flight goes from 0.19 s
        //     to ~0.45 s;
        //   - 250 is still 12.5 g and nothing physical: „nicht so physics accurate aber mehr
        //     haptisch" is the instruction, this is a weight the player can feel and not a
        //     simulation. LOWER = heavier and more sluggish; raise it back to 875 to get
        //     exactly the 2026-08-25 feel back.
        // ⚠️ The winch (Ctrl) is deliberately NOT capped — see reel_speed_m_s and FIND-172.
        drive_accel_max_m_s2: 250.0,
        // FIND-172. How much of the RADIAL pull survives while A or D is held, 0..1.
        //
        // „nur wenn ich a oder d druecke dass es staerker zur seite geht als rangezogen!"
        //   - raising drive_lateral_m_s past drive_speed_m_s does NOT do this: the two share
        //     one cap (locomotion::rope_drive), so a bigger lateral brakes the flight instead
        //     of turning it — Q-050's bug in a new key;
        //   - what is scaled is the rope axis's DIRECTION WEIGHT, never the target speed. With
        //     W+D held the target points atan(30 / (70*0.35)) = **50.7 deg** off the rope, and
        //     it is still driven at the full drive speed. Steering TURNS the drive, it does not
        //     slow it;
        //   - 0.35 makes the sideways component 1.22x the radial one at full W+D. With W
        //     released the radial pull is only drive_idle_speed_m_s and the ratio is 2.5x.
        //     LOWER = A/D wins harder; 1.0 is the 2026-08-25 behaviour, where the radial won.
        drive_steer_pull_fraction: 0.35,
        // FIND-172. THE ALWAYS-ON PULL, in m/s of closing speed along the rope.
        //
        // „ich will dass es immer ranzieht. nicht nur wenn ich w druecke!"
        //
        // ⚠️ THIS REVERSES FIND-149, AND ON PURPOSE. That finding recorded the reference doing
        // the opposite („wenn ich nichts drucke dann wird auch nicht rangezogen!") — his own
        // observation, and it still stands as an observation. He has now asked for the opposite
        // in THIS game, and his instruction beats his own earlier one (CLAUDE.md). Whoever
        // „restores" the old behaviour thinking this is a regression is undoing an instruction.
        //
        //   - it is the WINCH at a lower speed (locomotion::rope_winch): a FLOOR under the
        //     closing speed, so it can never brake, never touches the crossing momentum and
        //     stops at min_rope_m. Ctrl raises the floor to reel_speed_m_s (28) and costs gas;
        //     this one is free and always on. The two are one term, so holding Ctrl is exactly
        //     as strong as it was before this line existed (FIND-159, scripts/game-full.txt);
        //   - 12 m/s is 2x run_speed_m_s: a rope that carries you, not one that hauls you. On a
        //     VERTICAL rope the player settles at 12 - 20*0.35 = **5 m/s of climb** — under his
        //     own run speed, so hanging still is not faster than walking;
        //   - it runs only while player::locomotion::in_flight. A hooked player STANDING on the
        //     ground keeps his legs (and Ctrl is how he leaves the ground, F-005).
        drive_idle_speed_m_s: 12.0,
        // FIND-172. The time constant of the always-on pull, in seconds — deliberately NOT
        // drive_ramp_s. 0.08 s is the answer to „man macht was und man merkt es auch direkt",
        // and NOBODY PRESSES THIS ONE: it has to read as a rope taking up slack, not as a tug.
        // At 0.35 s the onset peaks at (12-0)/0.35 = 34 m/s^2 against the drive's 250, and the
        // climb it can hold against gravity is 12 - 20*0.35 = 5 m/s (see above). Bigger = softer
        // AND weaker against gravity; the two are the same number and that is not an accident.
        drive_idle_ramp_s: 0.35,
        // Gauss-Seidel iterations over both rope constraints. With one iteration the second
        // constraint violates the first again; two are enough in the normal case (two roofs,
        // angle over 60 degrees) for under 1 % residual error. The degenerate case — both
        // anchors nearly in one line — does not converge with two either (docs/interface.md).
        rope_iterations: 2,
        // ⚠️⚠️ THERE IS NO `gas_regen_per_s` AND NO `gas_regen_delay_s` IN THIS BLOCK, AND
        // THAT IS A DECISION — NOT SOMETHING SOMEBODY FORGOT. DO NOT ADD ONE.
        //
        // The user, 2026-08-12, closing docs/QUESTIONS.md Q-033: "gas refillt nur im main
        // gebaeude an bestimmten stationen/objekten". Gas comes back at a PLACE YOU GO TO,
        // never on a timer. Between 2026-08-10 and 2026-08-12 this file did carry a 10/s
        // regeneration behind a 0.5 s pause; he was asked which of three shapes he wanted and
        // picked none of them. The whole mechanism was removed with those two keys —
        // `refill_tank`, `arm_pause` and `Gas::regen_delay_left_s` went with it.
        //
        // Why he is right: the bible couples gas to RISK, not to a clock — burning it is loud
        // and a Bellower answers the noise (bible line 159). A tank that quietly fills itself
        // while you hang around on a roof is exactly the timer the bible refuses; a tank you
        // have to fly home to fill turns the whole map into a resource decision. The refuel
        // stations are a WORLD feature and they are queued in docs/NEXT.md §1d.
        //
        // If the tank feels tight before those exist, the knob is gas_tank / gas_boost_per_s
        // below, NOT a rate. `deny_unknown_fields` in src/data/mod.rs means either key put
        // back here crashes the game on load rather than being ignored, and
        // tests/vector_gas.rs::f018_an_idle_tank_never_refills_on_its_own goes red the moment
        // anything in the simulation puts a drop back.

        // 100.0 -> 300.0 -> 15000.0. **THE USER, 2026-08-20, and this is his third time
        // saying it:** „immernoch viel zu wenig gas. man kann nicht testen! mach das 50
        // fache!" — so 300 x 50, as asked, not a compromise at 10x. His instruction beats a
        // derivation and beats his own earlier number (CLAUDE.md; scale.ron and Q-002 already
        // encode that precedence). docs/QUESTIONS.md Q-046 records it as his decision.
        //
        // ⚠️ **THIS IS A TESTABILITY VALUE, NOT A BALANCE VALUE.** He said „man kann nicht
        // testen" — the tank is this big so a sortie cannot end for lack of gas while he is
        // trying to feel the movement. Nothing here claims 15000 is the shipped balance.
        // **The one-line rollback is `gas_tank: 300.0`** and nothing else has to move with it.
        //
        // At 18/s: **833.3 s of continuous held boost** (was 16.67 s at 300) against a 330 s
        // sortie — i.e. the tank now outlasts the mission by 2.5x, which is the point.
        // **The length of a boost is gas_tank / gas_boost_per_s and nothing else** — with no
        // refill anywhere, this number IS the flight time.
        //
        // ⚠️ **AND IT DOES NOT ANSWER THE BALANCE QUESTION — Q-044 stays open.** With the bill
        // honest (FIND-139) a 300 tank bought ~16.7 s of held boost against a 330 s sortie,
        // and **there is still no refuel station in the world**; the design's refill rule is
        // base-only and is HIS (Q-033). No burn rate closes a 20x gap — only the stations do.
        // Whoever tunes this back down has to build those first.
        gas_tank: 15000.0,
        gas_boost_per_s: 18.0,
        // For scale, the other consumer: reeling a full rope in from the 500 m anchor range
        // (scale.ron: vector.anchor_range_m) to the 3 m minimum takes (500-3)/28 = 17.75 s
        // and costs 106.5 gas — 0.71 % of a 15000 tank.
        //
        // ⚠️ This line said "from 90 m ... 3.1 s ... 18.6 gas — 6.2 % of the tank" until
        // 2026-08-20, and it was false on the RANGE too, not only on the tank: anchor_range_m
        // has been 500.0, not 90. Re-derived here rather than merely rebased.
        gas_reel_per_s: 6.0,
        // ⚠️ NEW ON 2026-08-13, AND NOTHING SPENDS IT YET. What the rope-pull thrust
        // (player.air_pull_m_s2 / air_lateral_m_s2) costs per second once `W4` bills it.
        //
        // **It exists because the thrust would otherwise be free**, and that was the one flaw
        // all nine judges of the §1B plan named independently: a `W` that pulls at 30 m/s²
        // with no meter moving is a stronger boost than the boost, for nothing, and the whole
        // game is „flight costs gas".
        //
        // The number is not chosen, it is SOLVED — from the same ratio that already decides
        // gas_dodge, gas per m/s of speed bought:
        //
        //     held boost   gas_boost_per_s / boost_m_s2    = 18 / 34 = 0.5294 gas per m/s
        //     rope steer   gas_steer_per_s / air_pull_m_s2 = 16 / 30 = 0.5333 gas per m/s
        //                                                   -------------------------------
        //     difference 0.0039 — the two thrusts cost the SAME per metre per second
        //
        // That is deliberate and it is what tests/data.rs pins (|delta| <= 0.15). The dodge is
        // the expensive one (1.875) and it is meant to be; the steer is not a third price
        // point, it is the boost's price on a different button. What a player therefore has to
        // learn is WHICH thrust the situation wants, never which one is cheap.
        //
        // In tank terms: 15000 gas is 937.5 s of held steering, a little longer than the
        // 833.3 s of held boost (18.75 s against 16.67 s at the old 300 tank; the RATIO —
        // steering outlasts the boost by 12.5 % — is what this line is about, and it is a
        // property of the two rates that survives any tank size) — and on an empty tank the rope term is ZERO (the look term keeps
        // air_accel_empty_fraction, §1e). ⚠️ UNTUNED. ⚠️ And "18.75 s of held steering" is the
        // WORST CASE and not the normal one: since FIND-139 a steering tick is only billed when
        // the rope really thrusts, which in the measured sortie was 11 ticks out of 543.
        //
        // Spent since 2026-08-13: GasConsumer::Steer, booked in src/vector/gas.rs and read by
        // player::locomotion::air_control.
        //
        // ⚠️ **THE RATE IS RIGHT AND THE BILL WAS WRONG — 2026-08-20, docs/FINDINGS.md FIND-139.**
        // The user, after playing: „gas ist VIEL zu schnell weg!" The ledger of one ordinary
        // sortie (scripts/f018-budget.txt, DBT_GAS_LEDGER=1) said where it went:
        //
        //     steer 144.8 (48.3 % of the tank)   boost 98.1   dodge 45.0   reel 12.1
        //
        // Steer was the LARGEST line item in the game, bigger than the boost he actually presses
        // — and it bought nothing. `rope_steer` multiplies the pull by `max(0, l̂ · r̂)`, a player
        // hangs UNDER his anchor and looks where he is going, so the delivered thrust over the
        // sortie's 99 sampled steer ticks was a mean of 0.0012 of air_pull_m_s2 (median 0.0000).
        // The want condition read the BUTTON — `anchored && (W || A || D)` — and charged 16/s
        // through all of it.
        //
        // **No number here changed.** The fix is `vector::gas::steer_has_effect`: the want is read
        // off the geometry, which is what "the cost follows the effect, not the button" meant all
        // along. Same binary, same script, after: steer 2.9 instead of 144.8, and the sortie ends
        // with 77 of 300 gas in hand instead of running dry.
        //   ⚠️ **Those are the numbers AS MEASURED ON 2026-08-20 against the 300 tank**, kept
        //   verbatim because they are the before/after record that justified the fix. Against
        //   today's 15000 tank the same sortie ends with 14777.2 in hand and every "% of the
        //   tank" above is 50x smaller; the ABSOLUTE figures (144.8, 98.1, 45.0, 12.1) and the
        //   ratios between them are what the finding is about, and those are unchanged.
        //
        // So the table above is now true of the thrust the player RECEIVES and not only of the
        // peak he was charged for — which is what made 16.0 the honest number in the first place.
        // Whoever lowers this rate to "fix" the tank is turning the wrong knob a second time
        // (docs/QUESTIONS.md Q-044), and whoever puts the button condition back re-opens FIND-139;
        // tests/vector_gas.rs::f006_the_steer_is_billed_exactly_when_the_rope_really_thrusts holds
        // both conditions against each other over 750 geometries and goes red on either.
        //
        // ⚠️ What it does NOT do: prorate. At a 4 m rope the fade delivers 8 % of the pull and the
        // full 16/s is still charged. Prorating needs `rope_steer`'s own `Σcᵢfᵢ/n` factor, and
        // there is no `vector -> player` edge in docs/architecture.md's allow list. FIND-139.
        gas_steer_per_s: 16.0,
        // F-008, and the ONE gas number in this block that is not a rate: what a single dodge
        // costs, billed once on the tick the double-tap lands. No `_per_s` in the name because
        // there is no per second about it — vector/gas.rs does NOT multiply it by dt.
        //
        // ⚠️ UNTUNED. The user, 2026-08-12 (docs/NEXT.md §1c): "mit doppel leertaste boostet
        // man stark in die lauf richtung (ein weiter dodge) DER VIEL GAS AUFBRAUCHT. das andere
        // boosten VERBRAUCHT SEHR WENIG!" So these two numbers have to be far apart in a way
        // somebody can feel, and the interesting question is: far apart measured how?
        //
        // NOT per second. 45 gas is 2.5 seconds of held boost, and "2.5 seconds" does not sound
        // expensive. The honest comparison is GAS PER m/s OF SPEED BOUGHT, because speed is
        // what both of them sell:
        //
        //     held boost   gas_boost_per_s / boost_m_s2     = 18 / 34 = 0.529 gas per m/s
        //     dodge        gas_dodge / dodge_impulse_m_s    = 45 / 24 = 1.875 gas per m/s
        //                                                    ---------------------------
        //     the dodge costs 3.54x more gas for the same m/s
        //
        // THAT is the ratio, and it is what makes gas_boost_per_s: 18 demonstrably the cheap
        // one (tests/vector_boost.rs::f008_the_dodge_is_the_expensive_boost_per_metre_per_second
        // holds it and names 3.0 as the floor). What the dodge buys for the premium is TIME: it
        // delivers its 24 m/s in ONE TICK, where the boost needs 24/34 = 0.71 s of held button
        // to reach the same speed — 42x faster, for 3.54x the gas. That is a dodge: you pay for
        // the instant, not for the speed.
        //
        // In tank terms: 15000 gas is 833.3 s of held boost OR 333 dodges.
        //
        // ⚠️ **AND THAT VOIDS AN ARGUMENT THIS NUMBER USED TO REST ON.** Until 2026-08-20 the
        // line read "300 gas is 16.67 s of held boost OR 6.67 dodges. Six dodges per run with
        // no station in the world yet is deliberately tight ... the gas price is the whole
        // limiter today". At 333 dodges per run **the gas price is no longer a limiter at
        // all**, so what keeps F-008 an emergency move rather than a traversal move is now
        // NOTHING — the backlog calls it "kurz, hart, mit eigenem Cooldown" and **the cooldown
        // is still not built** (FIND-067).
        //
        // That is a consequence of the 50x testability tank (see gas_tank above), not a
        // decision about the dodge. `gas_dodge: 45.0` is deliberately NOT lowered to
        // compensate: the per-m/s ratio below is what makes it the expensive one and it is
        // intact at any tank size. If the dodge starts being spammed as traversal, the fix is
        // the cooldown or the rollback to `gas_tank: 300.0` — docs/QUESTIONS.md Q-046.
        gas_dodge: 45.0,
        // Who pays first when the tank is not enough for both? That is a GAME-VALUE
        // decision and not an `if` in the code (docs/QUESTIONS.md Q-017).
        //
        // Dodge is APPENDED, not inserted, and that is a decision with a number behind it: the
        // order of the first two is the user's answer to Q-017 and nothing about F-008 revisits
        // it. And the position of Dodge in this list is worth almost nothing — a dodge costs 45
        // flat while boost+reel together cost 0.4 per tick, so being served last can cost a
        // dodge at most 0.4 gas, i.e. 0.9 % of its own price. Putting it first would look like a
        // decision and buy nothing.
        // Steer is INSERTED second and that position is a decision, not an append
        // (docs/QUESTIONS.md Q-037, and it is the user's to overturn). Steer and Boost are both
        // rates, both wanted on the very same tick of a swing, so on a nearly empty tank this
        // list decides which of the two the last drop buys. Boost stays first because it is the
        // deliberate press: Shift means "I need speed NOW", while W/A/D on a rope is held almost
        // continuously in flight — putting Steer first would let the ambient input eat the drop
        // the explicit one asked for, and an ignored Shift reads as a broken game.
        // F-009 Flip is APPENDED, exactly as Dodge was, and for the same arithmetic: it costs
        // 20 flat while the three rates together cost 0.4 in a tick, so its position can cost
        // it at most 2 % of its own price. Putting it in front of Boost would look like a
        // decision and would overturn the user's answer to Q-017 as a side effect of adding a
        // verb — docs/QUESTIONS.md Q-052 records that and the assumption it runs under.
        gas_priority: [Boost, Steer, ReelIn, Dodge, Flip],
        boost_m_s2: 34.0,
        // How much of the boost DIRECTION comes from the rope instead of from the look
        // direction: 0.0 = pure look direction (the behaviour up to 2026-08-10), 1.0 = straight
        // at the anchor. The blend is a direction only — the STRENGTH stays boost_m_s2 at every
        // value (tests/vector_boost.rs).
        //
        // ⚠️ UNTUNED, and the number the user has to feel rather than read. It exists because
        // he asked for it after playing it on 2026-08-10: „wenn man boostet soll man in
        // richtung seil und mauszeiger fliegen!"
        //
        // Why not 1.0, which is what „in richtung seil" says literally: thrust straight at the
        // anchor is RADIAL, and a taut rope eats a radial pull — it adds no tangential speed
        // and so it kills the swing instead of feeding it. Why not 0.0, which is what we had:
        // the rope then steers nothing at all. 0.5 is the middle where looking at your own hook
        // gives you nearly the whole pull while looking away still steers.
        //
        // What the rope half BUYS, and it is the reason this key exists at all: since B-005 the
        // enforced length ratchets down to the true distance every substep, so flying at your
        // anchor permanently SHORTENS the rope — and a shorter rope is what lifts the bottom of
        // the arc off the ground (measured in B-005: 53.3 m of rope -> 20.2 m moved the arc
        // bottom from 18.3 m underground to 14.8 m above it). Look-only boost cannot reach that
        // loop; this is what turns a dead leash into a swing.
        boost_rope_fraction: 0.5,
        // F-008. How much speed ONE dodge adds, in m/s. A VELOCITY CHANGE, not an acceleration:
        // vector/boost.rs divides it by the fixed timestep to get what avian wants, so this
        // number stays the same speed if simulation_hz ever moves, and it is checkable against
        // things a player knows — a pure swing runs 17-21 m/s (Q-018), max_speed_m_s is 75.
        //
        // ⚠️ UNTUNED. 24 m/s is chosen to be unmistakably "stark" and "ein weiter dodge": it is
        // MORE than a whole swing's speed added in one tick, so a dodge out of a 20 m/s swing
        // roughly doubles the speed and crosses a 7 m street in 0.3 s. Below about 12 it would
        // be a nudge and nobody would pay 45 gas for it; above about 40 the max_speed_m_s clamp
        // starts eating it whenever you dodge along your own velocity, which would make the
        // number quietly stop meaning what it says.
        //
        // ⚠️ The one consequence to know: for ONE tick this is 24 * 60 = 1440 m/s² of
        // acceleration, 42x boost_m_s2. That is what an impulse IS, and BoostAccel really does
        // carry that spike for that tick. Nothing reads BoostAccel today except the tests; the
        // day something does (a HUD "thrust" bar, a sound trigger), it has to expect it.
        dodge_impulse_m_s: 24.0,
        // F-008. How many TICKS may lie between the two Space presses for them to be one
        // double-tap. In ticks, because the tick counter is the clock that measures it —
        // Time<Virtual> is what --ticks and the seeded rng are held steady against and must not
        // be read for gameplay, and a window in seconds would only be divided back into ticks
        // with a rounding nobody can see.
        //
        // ⚠️ UNTUNED. 18 ticks = 0.300 s at simulation_hz: 60. Two bounds, and the lower one is
        // measured rather than felt:
        //   - a HUMAN double-tap is ~150-300 ms between presses, so much below ~12 ticks the
        //     move stops being reachable;
        //   - it MUST stay well under one jump, or bunny-hopping on the ground would fire an
        //     expensive dodge by accident. jump_speed_m_s 6.5 against gravity_m_s2 -20 is
        //     2*6.5/20 = 0.65 s of airtime = 39 ticks before Space can produce a second jump.
        //     18 is 46 % of that, so a ground double-jump attempt can NEVER land inside the
        //     window. (In the air the second tap is exactly what this is for.)
        dodge_double_tap_window_ticks: 18,
        // Velocity clamp from day 1, not retrofitted (bible 6.4, risk of fling exploits)
        //
        // ⚠️ Computed against the city of the size table, and the numbers do not fit
        // together (docs/QUESTIONS.md Q-018): pure swinging off an 11.5 m roof gives
        // 17-21 m/s, the windups of the titans stay readable up to 6-20 m/s, and 75 m/s
        // only comes out of a full reel-in along a clear 30 m line of sight. 75 m/s crosses
        // the 400 m graybox in 5.33 s and turns a 7 m street into a curve at 164 g and
        // 1228 degrees/s of yaw rate. The number stays standing anyway: it is a clamp
        // against exploits, not a target speed, and what the factor of x1.5 named by the
        // user refers to is open. It is not changed before there is an answer.
        max_speed_m_s: 75.0,

        // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
        // F-008 — WHAT BOUNDS A DASH, now that the gas price does not
        // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
        // Q-046 / FIND-152, measured 2026-08-20: the tank went 300 -> 15000 for testability, so
        // `gas_dodge: 45` went from 6.7 dashes per sortie to **333**. At 333 the price is not a
        // limiter, it is a rounding error, and the backlog row for F-008 says in so many words
        // *„mit eigenem Cooldown ... Anzahl der Dashes ist ein Stat"* — neither half existed.
        //
        // So the answer to "what now bounds a dash" is THESE THREE NUMBERS and not the gas:
        //
        //   dodge_charges     how many in a row      3
        //   dodge_recharge_s  how fast they return   4 s each -> 12 s for a full magazine
        //   dodge_cooldown_s  how fast they leave    0.6 s between two
        //
        // ⚠️ UNTUNED, and the three are chosen against each other rather than felt:
        //   - 3 is the smallest number that is a *magazine* and not a cooldown. At 2 the stat
        //     has one step in it; at 5 the burst is longer than the 0.6 s spacing can shape.
        //   - 0.6 s is 36 ticks — twice `dodge_double_tap_window_ticks` (18), so the gesture
        //     that fires a dash can never accidentally fire the next one, and a player drumming
        //     on Space gets exactly one dash per 36 ticks instead of one per 18.
        //   - 4 s per charge makes a burst of three cost 12 s of restraint. Against a 330 s
        //     sortie that is 27 full magazines, which is generous — the number is a **shape**,
        //     not a scarcity, and scarcity is `F-019`'s job.
        //
        // 🔴 What this does NOT do: bill anything. `gas_dodge: 45` is still charged on top, and
        // the charge gate sits IN FRONT of the bill (`vector::gas::gas_budget` reads
        // `DodgeCharges::ready`) — a dash refused for want of a charge costs nothing. That
        // ordering is FIND-152 read the right way round: the gate must not sit behind the money.
        dodge_charges: 3.0,
        dodge_recharge_s: 4.0,
        dodge_cooldown_s: 0.6,

        // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
        // F-009 — the sideways flip: „Doppeltipp A/D erzeugt seitlichen Ausweichsprung in der
        // Luft mit kurzen I-Frames."
        // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
        // ⚠️ UNTUNED. The flip is the ACROBATIC verb, not the fast one, and every number here
        // is set against the dash it must not replace:
        //
        //   dodge_impulse_m_s  24.0  costs 45 gas and a charge, goes where WASD points
        //   flip_impulse_m_s   18.0  costs 20 gas and NO charge, goes strictly sideways
        //
        // 18 < 24 on purpose: the flip is cheaper and always available, so it must buy less
        // distance, or nobody would ever pay for the dash. What it buys instead is the
        // i-frame window — the dash has none.
        flip_impulse_m_s: 18.0,
        // Sideways only would drive a flip into the wall you are trying to leave: hugging a
        // facade, `A` throws you along it and gravity has you back on it in a third of a
        // second. 6 m/s of lift is 1.8 m of rise at gravity_m_s2 -20, i.e. one body length —
        // enough to clear a window ledge, far short of a jump (jump_speed_m_s 6.5).
        flip_up_m_s: 6.0,
        // 0.35 s = 21 ticks. The acceptance sentence is *„Flip vermeidet einen Titanengriff,
        // wenn im Fenster ausgeloest"* — so this is a WINDOW, and it has to be short enough
        // that mistiming it is a real mistake. A titan's Strike lands on one tick
        // (`combat::strike::land` + `StrikeSpent`), so 21 ticks is 21 chances to be the one.
        flip_iframes_s: 0.35,
        // The same 18 ticks as the dash's window, and deliberately the same number: two
        // gestures on one keyboard that need different timings is a control scheme nobody
        // learns. If one moves, both move.
        flip_double_tap_window_ticks: 18,
        // Flat, never a rate — the same contract `gas_dodge` carries. 20 against the dash's 45:
        // per m/s bought that is 20/18 = 1.11 against 45/24 = 1.875, so the flip is the cheaper
        // impulse and the dash stays the expensive one (the ratio `tests/vector_boost.rs`
        // already holds against the boost).
        gas_flip: 20.0,
    ),

    camera: (
        // 90.0 -> 60.0. The user names "FOV ground combat 55-65 degrees" and calls it the
        // "biggest lever". The words GROUND COMBAT are the key: 60 degrees is the BASE on
        // the ground, not the upper limit. A 90-degree image makes every titan small and
        // every meter short — exactly what this game does not want.
        // Window: scale.ron:camera.min_ground_fov_deg/-max_ground_fov_deg, tests/data.rs checks it.
        //
        // ⚠️ The number is read VERTICALLY — src/render/mod.rs puts it into
        // `PerspectiveProjection.fov`, and Bevy documents the field as "the vertical
        // field of view". On 16:9 that is 91.5 degrees horizontal (before: 121.3). Whether
        // the user means his 55-65 degrees vertically or horizontally nobody has decided,
        // and the two readings differ by a factor of 1.7: docs/QUESTIONS.md Q-021.
        fov_deg: 60.0,
        // Field of view at max_speed_m_s. F-017 „Geschwindigkeits-Feedback" will later
        // interpolate between fov_deg and this value — the curve belongs in the code, the
        // two ends belong here.
        // ⚠️ STARTING VALUE, not from the user: 90 degrees is exactly the value that stood
        // here until 2026-08-09. At the top end the image thus stays as it was; only ground
        // combat pulls together. That is the smallest change that satisfies the requirement
        // — not an invented second number. F-017 names „abschaltbar fuer Motion Sickness";
        // whoever wants the effect off sets this value to fov_deg.
        fov_max_speed_deg: 90.0,
        // F-017 — where the speed curve starts, in m/s. Below it the image is exactly
        // `fov_deg` and F-017 is invisible; from here to `vector.max_speed_m_s` (75) it opens
        // linearly to `fov_max_speed_deg`.
        //
        // ⚠️ UNTUNED. 22 m/s is picked off a measurement and not off a feeling: a pure swing
        // runs 17-21 m/s (Q-018), so at 22 the lens is still at rest while you are merely
        // swinging, and it starts to open the moment the drive or a dash puts you above what
        // a rope alone can do. Walking (run_speed_m_s) is nowhere near it, which is the point
        // — a walk that widens the lens sells nothing.
        //
        // The 22 -> 75 span is 53 m/s wide and covers 30 degrees of FOV, i.e. 0.57 deg per m/s.
        fov_speed_from_m_s: 22.0,
        mouse_deg_per_px: 0.08,
        pitch_limit_deg: 89.0,
        smoothing_half_life_s: 0.05,
    ),

    world: (
        // Edge length of one grid cell of the spatial index (T-036a).
        // ⚠️ UNMEASURED. docs/lessons/performance.md says it literally: "§11 says
        // ‚Gitterzellen', not how big. Has to be measured." A 90 m ray at 8 m crosses
        // about 11 cells horizontally and 15 vertically at the wall
        // (docs/QUESTIONS.md **Q-014** — until 2026-08-09 a reference to Q-013 stood here,
        // and Q-013 is the question about the maximum rope length).
        cell_m: 8.0,
        // Half edge length of the grid. Has to cover the map AND a full hook range
        // beyond it.
        // 320.0 -> 300.0: recomputed with the new range. 400/2 + 90 = 290 -> 300
        // (rounded up to a round number). The 320 belonged to 112 m (400/2 + 112 = 312).
        // In HEIGHT the same number covers wall (120 m) + Ashwalker (150 m) = 270 m with
        // 30 m to spare. Whether the grid is three-dimensional at all is decided by Q-014;
        // tests/data.rs pins the 270 down anyway, because a grid smaller than the world
        // would be wrong either way.
        //
        // **300 -> 400 on 2026-08-10**, forced by `hook_range_m` 90 -> 200 (Q-035): the grid
        // has to carry half the map plus one full hook range, and `tests/data.rs` fires the
        // moment it does not — 400/2 + 200 = **400 exactly**, no rounding left over.
        // The cost is real and is written here so nobody has to re-derive it: the grid covers
        // 800 x 800 m instead of 600 x 600 at `cell_m: 8.0`, i.e. **10 000 cells against
        // 5 625 — 1.78x the index memory** for the same city. It buys nothing by itself; it
        // is the price of the longer rope. If the index ever becomes a memory problem, the
        // lever is `cell_m`, not this number, and it is measured in
        // docs/lessons/performance.md before it is touched.
        // **400 -> 600 on 2026-08-12**, to make room for a real district. The guard is
        // `half_extent_m >= longest_map_edge/2 + hook_range_m`, so 600 buys a map of up to
        // **800 x 800 m** at the current 200 m range — the graybox's 400 m cleared it with
        // exactly zero margin and could not have grown by a metre.
        // Cost, stated so nobody re-derives it: the grid covers 1200 x 1200 m instead of
        // 800 x 800 at `cell_m: 8.0`, i.e. **22 500 cells against 10 000 — 2.25x the index
        // memory**. That is the price of a district with a 120 m wall in it, and it buys
        // nothing on its own.
        // **600 -> 900 on 2026-08-13**, and this one is forced by arithmetic, not chosen: the
        // guard is `half_extent_m >= longest_map_edge/2 + hook_range_m`, ashgate is 700 m
        // across and the range is 500 now, so the floor is 350 + 500 = **850**. 900 is that
        // floor plus 50 m — enough that the next block placed at the district's edge does not
        // move this number again, not so much that the grid pays for a map nobody built.
        //
        // The cost, MEASURED and not estimated this time (2026-08-13, machine B, 8 interleaved
        // headless runs of 900 ticks on ashgate, A/B/A/B so that background load hits both
        // sides): the grid covers 1800 x 1800 m instead of 1200 x 1200 at `cell_m: 8.0`, i.e.
        // **50 625 cells against 22 500 — 2.25x again**, and it costs **+0.09 %** of process
        // CPU (6.174 s -> 6.180 s, best run of four each; the means are 6.847 -> 6.612 and
        // that -3.4 % is another agent's render job, not a speed-up).
        //
        // WHY it is free, and this is the part worth keeping: `SpatialIndex::new` allocates
        // `columns²` cells **once at startup and never per tick** (src/shared/spatial.rs says
        // so in the field's own comment), and `maintain_index` touches only bodies that moved.
        // So 2.25x the cells is 1.2 MB of empty `Vec` headers at load and nothing at 60 Hz.
        // What would NOT be free is `cell_m`, because that changes how many cells every ray
        // walks — it stays 8.0, it is unmeasured (docs/QUESTIONS.md Q-014), and it does not
        // get touched on a hunch.
        //
        // ⚠️ Note for whoever measures this next: in `--headless` the frame time is NOT the
        // metric. `ScheduleRunnerPlugin::run_loop(1/240 s)` paces the loop, so DBT_FRAMETIME
        // reads 4.221 ms/frame before AND after — that is the cap, not the work. Process CPU
        // over a fixed tick count is what moves.
        half_extent_m: 900.0,
        // Whoever occupies more cells lands in the linear large-body list instead of in the
        // grid. The ground (400 x 400 m) occupies 2500 cells at 8 m cells; every horizontal
        // ray would otherwise test it once per visited cell.
        large_body_cells: 64,
        // The thinnest wall there may be. Calibrates max_substep_m.
        // ⚠️ In NO source: models.ron A-080 names a 4-stud grid, that is the module width,
        // not the thickness (docs/QUESTIONS.md Q-015).
        min_wall_m: 0.5,
        // Collision margin: the distance at which a body comes to a stop in front of a
        // surface. Without it every contact jitters, and the number lands as 1e-3 in Rust.
        collision_margin_m: 0.01,
    ),
    // The session (docs/multiplayer.md). ⚠️ UNTUNED, and two of the four are not free
    // inventions: slot_hold_s is the bible's own 120 s (F-158a), and the port is only a
    // default — --host <port> overrides it.
    net: (
        // 34197 is not lucky: it is out of the ephemeral range and out of the way of
        // everything a desktop usually has open.
        port: 34197,
        // One squad, not one stack. The deployment pads in the hub are 6 m across.
        seat_spread_m: 3.0,
        // Five seconds of silence is a dropped line, not a stutter. A client that sends one
        // packet per tick has missed 300 of them by then.
        peer_timeout_s: 5.0,
        // Bible F-158a, and it is a spec and not a taste: the seat stays his for two minutes.
        slot_hold_s: 120.0,
    ),
)
